## Why This Is Asked
This tests understanding of inter-process sharing via mmap, page cache semantics, and memory visibility without user-space locking.

## Key Concepts
- MAP_SHARED page mappings
- Page cache dirty bits and writeback
- Cache coherence and TLB effects
- Lightweight synchronization in shared memory (CAS, acquire/release)

## Code Example
```c
// Pseudo: writer uses CAS to acquire lock, writes, then releases
uint32_t *lock = (uint32_t*)addr; // shared region
while (__sync_lock_test_and_set(lock, 1)) {}
// write 8 bytes to region after lock
memcpy((void*)(addr+8), data, 8);
__sync_lock_release(lock);

// Reader checks lock before reading
while (*lock) {} // wait for writer
memcpy(data, (void*)(addr+8), 8);
```

## Tradeoffs
Lock: Simple, ensures exclusive access but blocks readers during writes.
Seqlock: Readers can proceed concurrently but must retry if write occurs mid-read; better for read-heavy workloads.

## Why This Is Asked

Tests understanding of memory cache eviction with per-process quotas, correctness under concurrency, and interaction with remote backends.

## Key Concepts

- Per-process quotas and accounting
- Per-page state (clean/dirty, eviction-claimed, evicted) and CAS-based races
- Eviction flow: candidate selection, quota enforcement, writeback coordination
- Data structures: per-CPU LRU, global eviction gate, writeback queues
- Remote store latency impact and fairness guarantees

## Code Example

```c
// Minimal eviction sketch
typedef struct page {
  atomic_int ref;
  int owner;                  // pid
  atomic_bool dirty;
  atomic_bool evicted;
  atomic_bool claimed;
} page_t;

bool evict_candidate(page_t *p, int owner_quota) {
  if (atomic_load(&p->dirty)) return false;      // never evict dirty before writeback
  if (atomic_load(&p->claimed)) return false;    // another thread claiming it
  if (atomic_compare_exchange_strong(&p->claimed, 0, 1)) {
    // update owner quota here atomically (pseudo)
    atomic_store(&p->evicted, 1);
    return true;
  }
  return false;
}
```

